      "subject": "elective",
      "material": "terms",
      "term": "Handling Class Imbalance",
      "definition": "In a class-imbalanced dataset, one label is considerably more common than the other.\nSolution:\n[STEP 1] Downsample the majority class: you shrink the majority class down to be closer in size to the minority class.\n[STEP 2] Upweighting: Instead of duplicating the X CLASS images back, you tell the model: \"every time you see an X CLASS example, treat it as if it were worth Y Value examples, not 1\"",
      "pdfWording": "In a class-imbalanced dataset, one label is considerably more common than the other.\nSolution:\n[STEP 1] Downsample the majority class: you shrink the majority class down to be closer in size to the minority class.\n[STEP 2] Upweighting: Instead of duplicating the X CLASS images back, you tell the model: \"every time you see an X CLASS example, treat it as if it were worth Y Value examples, not 1\"",
      "contentKind": "slide-excerpt",
      "questionStyle": "identify",
      "questionExcerpt": "In a class-imbalanced dataset, one label is considerably more common than the other.\nSolution:\n[STEP 1] Downsample the majority class: you shrink the majority class down to be closer in size to the minority class.\n[STEP 2] Upweighting: Instead of duplicating the X CLASS images back, you tell the model: \"every time you see an X CLASS example, treat it as if it were worth Y Value examples, not 1\"",
      "detail": "These are the slide’s proposed techniques, not a mandatory sequence for every imbalanced dataset.",
